-- title:
--   Mod p representation of a semistable model is ordinary
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring, $k$ a field which is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, $W$ a Weierstrass curve over $\mathbb{Z}$, and $p$ a prime with $p \neq 2$, together with a ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb{Z}/p \to k$. Assume: $\Delta_W \neq 0$; $W$ satisfies the project's semistability predicate [`WeierstrassCurve.IsSemistableModel`](def/FLTPrelim_Modularity.html#L85), i.e. for every prime $q$ with $q \mid \Delta_W$ one has $q \nmid c_4(W)$; the ordinarity alternative `hord`, namely either $p \mid \Delta_W$, or there is an index $i$ with $1 \le i < (p^2-1)/2$ such that the $i$-th coefficient of the auxiliary division polynomial `W.preΨ' p` is not divisible by $p$; the $p$-torsion of the group of points of $W_{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `W.map (Int.castRingHom ℚ)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has cardinality exactly $p^2$; and the Galois action on that $p$-torsion, given by `galoisRepModuleEnd`, satisfies [`GaloisFactorsThroughFiniteLevel`](def/GaloisRep_Residual.html#L17), i.e. there is a finite extension $L/\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that every automorphism fixing $L$ pointwise acts as the identity. Finally let $S$ be a finite set of naturals with $p \in S$ such that every prime $q \notin S$ is good for $W$ in the sense $q \nmid \Delta_W$. The conclusion is that the two-dimensional $k$-representation obtained from $W_{\mathbb{Q}}[p]$ (viewed as a [`ResidualGaloisRep`](def/GaloisRep_Residual.html#L22) over $\mathbb{Z}/p$), base changed along $\iota$ and regarded as a [`GaloisRepAdic`](def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L16) over the local ring $k$, satisfies [`GaloisRep.ordinaryCondition 𝒪 p S`](def/GaloisRep_LocalConditions.html#L28): that is, (i) $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $k$ (so $k$ has characteristic $p$) and for all $n$, $\sigma$ and $a$, if $\sigma$ acts on $p^n$-th roots of unity by raising to the $a$-th power then $\det \rho(\sigma) - a$ lies in the ideal generated by $p^n$; (ii) ordinarity at $p$: for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit in $P$ there is a line $L \subseteq V$ spanned by a member of some $k$-basis of $V$, stable under the decomposition subgroup of $P$, with inertia acting trivially on $V/L$; and (iii) unramifiedness at every prime $q \notin S$, i.e. $\rho(\sigma) = 1$ for all $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of any valuation subring lying over $q$. Since the maximal ideal of the field $k$ is zero, the congruences in (i) are equalities for $n \ge 1$.
--
--   This is the curve-side verification that the representation on the $p$-torsion of a semistable model, with its coefficients extended to a field $k$ of characteristic $p$, lies in the ordinary local deformation condition of type $(\mathrm{ord},\Sigma)$ used by Wiles. It differs from the textbook formulation in two ways: the ordinarity input is recorded in the division-polynomial (Hasse-invariant) form `hord` rather than as a condition on $a_p$, and the conclusion is exactly the three-part predicate [`GaloisRep.ordinaryCondition`](def/GaloisRep_LocalConditions.html#L28) (cyclotomic determinant, an ordinary line at $p$, unramifiedness outside $S$) for the given finite set $S$, with no claim about deformation rings, tangent spaces or modularity. It is used as the hypothesis "$\bar\rho$ itself satisfies the condition" in the non-emptiness statement for ordinary deformation data attached to $W$, in the construction of patching data for Hecke algebras at level $N$, and as a conjunct of the corresponding statement for the strict ordinary condition when $p \mid \Delta_W$.
